{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Biological evolution selects for reproduction (future existence), whereas Popperian selection chooses a theory to potentially destroy it (future falsification).","A gambler betting on a streak assumes the streak continues (induction); a Popperian choosing a theory assumes it might fail soon (deductive openness)."],"overturn_conditions":["If it can be shown that no rational agent would act on a theory without some expectation of its future reliability.","If the 'methodological rule' inevitably generates psychological belief in probability despite logical prohibitions."],"shared_structure":["Selection of a candidate based on past performance metrics.","Distinction between explanatory power and predictive certainty.","Rejection of probabilistic weighting in favor of binary survival status."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_003","SRC_007"],"transfer_claims":["Just as a biologist studies the most robust organism to understand limits of life, a scientist tests the most corroborated theory to understand limits of truth.","Preference does not imply expectation of continued success, only recognition of current resilience."]},"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The preference for better-corroborated theories is defensible on Popper's own terms without smuggling in induction, provided the preference is interpreted strictly as a methodological rule for selecting the most informative target for falsification (the 'best test object'), rather than as an epistemic bet on future predictive success. The cost of this defense is the abandonment of any guarantee that acting on the preferred theory will yield practical success; it accepts that the 'fittest' theory may be falsified tomorrow, rendering the action based on it retroactively irrational in outcome, though methodologically sound at the moment of choice.","counterconditions":["If the agent implicitly assumes the uniformity of nature to expect the preferred theory to work in the future.","If the preference is justified by an appeal to 'verisimilitude' (truth-likeness) given the Tichý-Miller theorem undermines its calculability.","If the distinction between 'acting on' a theory and 'believing' it to be probable collapses in practice."],"discharges":[],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Methodological Selection for Severity: The mechanism treats corroboration not as a measure of truth probability, but as a historical record of a theory's capacity to survive severe tests. The scientist prefers the better-corroborated theory because it has proven itself a harder nut to crack, offering the highest potential informational yield if it fails. This is a strategic choice for efficiency in error elimination, not an inductive inference that past survival predicts future survival.","optional_refs":[],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["23eed044f5c987a0e96292fdc3168ea1ae3455994a6e5d26d6b204a808f28404","674033b652ec81d91033cb694309478c575ba7831ee557afa9e850584e1c3fde"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.65}],"simulation_proposals":[]}